  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "For $a\\in\\mathbb R$, let $P(x)=x^3+ax^2+ax+1$. Find the value(s) of $a$ for which $P(x)$ has exactly:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q7-part-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "one distinct real root",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-1<a\\leq3$.\n\nFactor out $x+1$ and check the quadratic discriminant and repeated roots.",
          "worked": "Since $P(-1)=0$, the Factor Theorem gives\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=(x+1)\\bigl(x^2+(a-1)x+1\\bigr).\\\\ \\Delta&=(a-1)^2-4\\\\ \\Delta&=(a-3)(a+1).\\end{align*}$$\nFor $-1<a<3$, the quadratic has no real roots, leaving only $x=-1$. At $a=3$, $P(x)=(x+1)^3$, again giving only one distinct real root. Thus $-1<a\\leq3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q7-part-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "two distinct real roots",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=-1$.\n\nHere $P(x)=(x+1)(x-1)^2$.",
          "worked": "By the Factor Theorem,\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=(x+1)\\bigl(x^2+(a-1)x+1\\bigr).\\\\ \\Delta&=(a-1)^2-4\\\\ \\Delta&=(a-3)(a+1).\\end{align*}$$\nThe quadratic has a repeated root when $a=-1$ or $a=3$. For $a=-1$, $P(x)=(x+1)(x-1)^2$, with distinct real roots $-1$ and $1$. For $a=3$, $P(x)=(x+1)^3$, with only one distinct real root. Thus $a=-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q7-part-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "three distinct real roots",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a<-1$ or $a>3$.\n\nThe quadratic factor has two distinct real roots, neither equal to $-1$.",
          "worked": "By the Factor Theorem,\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=(x+1)\\bigl(x^2+(a-1)x+1\\bigr).\\\\ \\Delta&=(a-1)^2-4\\\\ \\Delta&=(a-3)(a+1).\\end{align*}$$\nThe quadratic has two distinct real roots when $\\Delta>0$, so $a<-1$ or $a>3$. Substituting $x=-1$ into the quadratic factor gives $3-a$, which is non-zero in these intervals. Its two roots therefore differ from the existing root $-1$, giving three distinct real roots.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      }
    ]
  },
